c-sponsored, August) shows Hatcher up 48-40, though polling is thin", "Molder's $2.6M-to-$720K fundraising edge and outside attacks on Hatcher keep this short of Safe R"], "rationale": "The district's ten-point Republican lean, Hatcher's consolidation of the party after ousting Ogles with Trump's subsequent endorsement, and near-unanimous Safe/Solid R ratings outweigh Molder's large cash advantage and a single August poll showing an eight-point race; markets and raters align on Molder as a clear underdog.", "watch": "Sustained outside spending against Hatcher, already at $273,000, or a late Democratic national wave could tighten the race, while smooth GOP consolidation would push it toward Safe R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Andy Ogles (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tennessee's 5th District features an open seat after Republican Andy Ogles lost renomination to former state Agriculture Commissioner Charlie Hatcher, who then received Trump's endorsement. Democrat Chaz Molder is the party's nominee in a district that leans Republican by roughly ten points.\n\nThe evidence points clearly toward Hatcher. Four major raters call the race Safe R and three call it Solid R, with the rest rating it Likely R. The only poll, conducted by a Democratic-aligned firm in August, showed Hatcher ahead 48 to 40 among likely voters. Polymarket prices Molder's chances at about seven percent. Molder's financial edge, with $2.6 million raised to Hatcher's $720,000, plus outside groups attacking Hatcher, and a national climate favoring Democrats give him a path, but the district's underlying lean makes him a clear underdog.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.079, "margin": -8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0675, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0985, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0593, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0597, "